WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.969
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:01.969 --> 00:00:09.269
On the 26th September 1983, in the midst of
the Cold War, a Russian Colonel named Stanislav

00:00:09.269 --> 00:00:16.270
Petrov was sat in the soviet missile control
centre monitoring incoming satellite data,

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:18.949
as he did every day.

00:00:18.949 --> 00:00:24.430
His job was to monitor the nuclear missile
early warning system and report a suspected

00:00:24.430 --> 00:00:28.910
launch higher up the command so they can retaliate.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:36.210
But on this day something happened for the
very first time, something he had been waiting

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.359
for, yet dreading, his entire career.

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:46.430
The alarm sounded, the early warning system
had detected that a nuclear missile had been

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:51.199
launched from the United States, heading towards
the USSR.

00:00:51.199 --> 00:00:57.230
Then, moments later, more warnings appeared,
five more missiles had been launched by the

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:58.230
US.

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:03.089
But Petrov sat there and did nothing.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:09.640
He didn't report it higher up the command
because he knew that would mean a full-scale

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:16.869
nuclear retaliation from Russia and the end
of the world as we know it.

00:01:16.869 --> 00:01:23.600
For he had a gut instinct, he felt that the
computers were malfunctioning and the warnings

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.969
were no more than false alarms.

00:01:26.969 --> 00:01:28.359
He was right.

00:01:28.359 --> 00:01:33.450
He went on to fix the bugs in the system that
caused the false alarm.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:39.880
His gut instinct saved the world and he has
thus come to be known as "The man who saved

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:41.390
the world".

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.829
He received no reward from the Soviets for
his actions.

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:51.719
He was in fact fired from his position and
re-assigned to a less sensitive post.

00:01:51.719 --> 00:01:56.939
Petrov believes his superiors were embarrassed
about there failings within the early warning

00:01:56.939 --> 00:01:58.429
system.

00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:03.859
People often tell you to trust your gut instinct,
but what exactly does that mean?

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:07.889
Do you ever see someone and instantly get
an uneasy feeling in your stomach?

00:02:07.889 --> 00:02:13.190
A little voice inside you that says that person
is up to no good.

00:02:13.190 --> 00:02:17.239
When you're driving you just know the person
to the side of you is going to suddenly change

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:20.500
lanes, before they even begin to move.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.040
The moment right before something bad happens
or you receive bad news you get a sinking

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:30.790
feeling in your stomach, a distressing anxiety
and tenseness.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:37.269
Well what if I said that this feeling is caused
by your second brain.

00:02:37.269 --> 00:02:43.769
That's right, some experts believe we have
two brains and one of them is in our stomach.

00:02:43.769 --> 00:02:48.390
Now we're not talking about a traditional
brain here, it certainly doesn't look like

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.390
one.

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:55.209
The brain and the gut are intricately connected
by a network of neurons and nerves, these

00:02:55.209 --> 00:03:01.470
information superhighways transport chemicals
and hormones between our brain and our stomach.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:05.450
It's how you know when you're hungry, your
stomach tells your brain.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.560
This connection is called the brain-gut axis.

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:14.959
But it's much more than a connection between
your brain and gut, this chemical highway

00:03:14.959 --> 00:03:21.250
is part of a larger network of hundreds of
millions of nerves and neurons, called the

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.670
enteric nervous system.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:29.530
It monitors your entire gastrointestinal system
from the oesophagus all the way down to your

00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:30.770
anus.

00:03:30.770 --> 00:03:37.099
It reports to the brain when we should feel
hungry, full, when we're ill, nauseous and

00:03:37.099 --> 00:03:38.099
more.

00:03:38.099 --> 00:03:45.180
But this is more than a fancy monitoring system,
research has proven that the enteric nervous

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.519
system really can act as a second brain.

